# Ignore everything without an extension, then selectively allow files and directories.
*
!*.*
!*/

# Don't ignore makefiles and license files.
!makefile
!Makefile
!LICENSE
!LICENSE-CODE

# Ignore GHDL work libraries and simulation artifacts. The rules above re-include anything
# with an extension, so every generated file type has to be named here explicitly.
work/
work-obj*.cf
*.ghw
*.vcd
*.fst
*.o
e~*

# Ignore Quartus build artifacts.
db/
incremental_db/
output_files/
greybox_tmp/
*.qws
*.rpt
*.summary
*.smsg
*.sld
*.done
*.qarlog

# Ignore the Python environment and bytecode used to redraw the diagrams.
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Ignore IDE directories.
.vscode/
.vs/
.claude/

# Ignore temp/ directory.
temp/

# Ignore solution modules written into an exercise directory, keeping the testbenches we ship.
#
# An exercise directory holds the testbench and nothing else: the module is the reader's to write,
# and shipping it early is the one thing the course design rules out. Solving an exercise locally,
# to check the testbench we hand out actually accepts a correct answer, leaves that solution
# sitting in the working tree where a single `git add -A` would publish it. These two lines make
# "not pushed" structural rather than a thing to remember.
#
# Already-tracked files are unaffected, so every shipped *_tb.vhd stays tracked either way. If a
# future exercise ever genuinely needs to ship a design file, `git add -f` overrides this.
lectures/*/exercises/*/*.vhd
!lectures/*/exercises/*/*_tb.vhd
